#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:55
msgid "Copy is the default for a reason"
msgstr ""

#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:58
msgid ""
"`file_operation` defaults to `\"copy\"`, not `\"move\"` — verified directly "
"that the original downloaded file survives an `organize()` call. A grouping "
"bug that also deleted your only copy of an already-downloaded scene would be"
" a serious, unrecoverable failure mode. Opt into `\"move\"` explicitly once "
"you trust your own grouping configuration, or use `\"symlink\"` to avoid "
"duplicating large scenes on disk while keeping the original flat layout "
"intact too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:66
msgid ""
"`organize()` is also idempotent — running it twice on the same input doesn't"
" re-copy, error, or double-count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:69
msgid "The manifest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:71
msgid "`manifest.json` maps every organized scene back to its metadata:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:94
msgid ""
"`OrganizeReport` (returned by `organize()`) also reports what *couldn't* be "
"organized, rather than silently dropping it: `skipped_no_output` (a "
"successful download with no file path attached) and "
"`skipped_failed_download` (the download itself failed) are both real, "
"separate counts — inspect them if `report.grouped` is lower than you expect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:101
msgid "Preparing an InSAR stack"
msgstr ""

#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:115
msgid ""
"`prepare_insar_stack()` groups downloaded SLCs by track/relative orbit and "
"flags orphaned scenes (no `relative_orbit` metadata at all, so they can't be"
" placed in any track)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:119
msgid ""
"When you supply orbit files and a ground point, it goes further: it reuses "
"[`pygeofetch.insar.stack_selection.select_burst_synchronized_dates`](../processing/sar-"
"insar/insar.md) — the same already-verified burst-timing classification the "
"InSAR pipeline itself uses — rather than a second, independent "
"implementation of that check. `report.ready_paths` then only includes the "
"dates that actually pass burst-sync classification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:126
msgid "Behaviour without orbit files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../home/claude/work/docs_audit/docs_out/data-access/data-organizer.md:129
msgid ""
"Without orbit files, burst-sync compatibility cannot be checked, because "
"orbit files are a separate download step. `prepare_insar_stack()` still "
"returns the track and date grouping, but `report.burst_sync_checked` is "
"`False` and `report.ready_paths` includes every date, not only the burst-"
"sync-compatible ones. Check `burst_sync_checked` before treating the stack "
"as ready for coregistration."
msgstr ""
